<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crimean Karaites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krymkaraylar, Karaims</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crimean Karaites are a Turkic-speaking community of the Karaite faith, a branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> that accepts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> but rejects rabbinic tradition. Long settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, they lived especially at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chufut-kale">Chufut-Kale</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Feodosia</a> and Yevpatoria, speaking the Karaim language. Under Russian rule after 1783 they gained a legal status separate from that of Rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crimean-karaites.jpg" alt="Crimean Karaites" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Raffet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qaraylar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
